sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch15-09> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The vertical outlines of regular sea-urchin shells are the forms of liquid drops depressed by gravity (with the pull of the tube-feet), their degree of flattening varying with the tension or strength of the shell: strong shells (Palaeechinus) stay nearly spherical, soft flexible ones (Phormosoma) are most flattened.";
    dc:type "mathematical-law";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Sea-urchin shell outlines match liquid drops flattened by gravity";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter XV, “On the Shapes of Eggs, and of Certain Other Hollow Structures”; topic “Form of sea-urchins”. Scope: tests of regular echinoids"
}
